[5] Assumption challenge and next proof moves
  alternate vertex sets considered:
    curves, quotient surfaces, diagonal forms, Frobenius twists, subgroup indices, supersingular prime residues, LRC runners, gaps, fixed circle sections, section boundaries, wall-crossing events, Pisano classes, code supports, matroid circuits, and proof obligations
  chosen quotient:
    proof-route vertices.  It preserves whether a scalar obstruction has a retained side channel and whether the channel is computable.
  information destroyed:
    actual surface geometry, explicit Magma group data, code support realizability, and multi-stranger LRC interactions.
  challenged assumption:
    do not treat supersingularity, Gleason positivity, or q-blocking as the obstruction.  In all three domains the proof-bearing object is the extra support channel retained after the scalar quotient.
  concrete next moves:
    (a) use the D7 index 78 as a design-ledger search seed for the 5-(72,16,78) minimum layer;
    (b) use the D6/A4 index 91 as the geometric analogue of the LRC14 q=91 fibered rescue;
    (c) model LRC14 Q27 descent after Church's partial-Frobenius descent: each blocked shell should either lower a degree/resource or land in a finite exceptional type.

